  <section class="tale-hero" id="top"><div class="tale-hero-copy"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/stories.html">Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Indra and Vritra</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">RIGVEDA / HYMN 1.32</p><h1>Break the restraint.<br><em>Let the waters move.</em></h1><p class="tale-deck">A hymn of storm, obstruction and release: Indra takes up the vajra, Vritra holds the waters behind the mountains, and the closed courses open toward the sea.</p><div class="tale-meta"><span>Rigveda · 1.32</span><span>About {{ storyCatalog.indra.minutes }} min read</span><a href="/sources.html#indra-vritra">View sources</a></div><a class="button" href="#deeds">Begin the hymn</a></div><figure class="tale-hero-art"><img src="/assets/images/indra-vritra-hero.png" alt="A devotional interpretation of two-armed Indra raising a golden vajra before the serpent-like Vritra as rivers break through storm-dark mountains" width="1536" height="864"><figcaption>INDRA CONFRONTS VRITRA · AI-GENERATED DEVOTIONAL INTERPRETATION</figcaption></figure></section>
  <div class="tale-introduction"><p>From the <strong>Rigveda</strong>.</p></div>
  <section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE FIGURES</p><h2 id="characters-title">Deity, restrainer and released waters</h2><dl><div><dt>Indra</dt><dd>The Vedic deva praised in this hymn for strength, the vajra and the opening of the waters’ paths.</dd></div><div><dt>Vritra</dt><dd>The powerful restrainer, also called <em>ahi</em>, whose hold closes the waters within the mountain passages.</dd></div><div><dt>The waters</dt><dd>Long confined, then described in motion toward their courses and the sea.</dd></div><div><dt>Vajra</dt><dd>Indra’s thunderbolt weapon, fashioned for the encounter and repeatedly named in the hymn.</dd></div></dl></section>
  <div class="tale-layout"><aside class="tale-contents"><p class="eyebrow">IN THIS HYMN</p><nav aria-label="Story chapters"><a href="#deeds"><span>01</span>The deeds to be told</a><a href="#weapon"><span>02</span>The vajra is fashioned</a><a href="#approach"><span>03</span>Indra approaches</a><a href="#restraint"><span>04</span>The restrainer of waters</a><a href="#release"><span>05</span>The passages open</a><a href="#resistance"><span>06</span>Thunder against thunder</a><a href="#question"><span>07</span>The hymn turns to a question</a><a href="#sovereignty"><span>08</span>The closing praise</a><a href="#traditions">Text &amp; interpretation</a><a href="/sources.html#indra-vritra">Sources &amp; reading notes</a></nav><a class="tale-back" href="/stories.html">Return to the collection</a></aside><article class="tale-body">{% include "reader-tools.njk" %}
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="deeds"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>The deeds to be told</h2></div><p>The hymn begins by announcing the heroic deeds of Indra. Its central action is given at once: he strikes the serpent-like restrainer, opens the waters and cleaves passages through the mountains.</p><p>This is not a court chronicle or a later biography. It is praise-poetry shaped around remembered divine action. The landscape is cosmic and immediate at the same time—storm, rock, river and power gathered into one song.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="weapon"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>The vajra is fashioned</h2></div><p>The divine artisan Tvashtar fashions the vajra for Indra. The weapon belongs to the thunder-bearing force of the scene, but the hymn’s attention quickly moves beyond the blow itself.</p><p>Once the obstruction is broken, the waters hurry onward like cows moving toward a calf. The comparison gives the released rivers direction and urgency: what was held back now knows its course.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="approach"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>Indra approaches</h2></div><p>Indra drinks Soma, chooses the vajra and advances against Vritra. The hymn gathers preparation, strength and decision into a few swift verses.</p><p>Vritra is not treated as a minor obstacle. He is the first and greatest of the serpentine restrainers in this praise, wrapped around the mountain world and keeping its life-giving movement closed.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="restraint"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>The restrainer of waters</h2></div><p>The encounter is described through shattered obstruction. Vritra’s powers fail; sun, dawn and sky become visible; the one who enclosed the waters falls like a tree cut down.</p><p>Further verses intensify the image. The wounded restrainer lies across the courses, and the hymn names Danu, his mother, beside him. The language is compressed and formidable, refusing the smooth sequence of a modern battle scene.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="release"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>05</span><h2>The passages open</h2></div><p>The waters move over and beyond the fallen obstruction. They had stood confined like cattle behind an enclosure; Indra opens the barred passages and their current resumes.</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/indra-vritra-waters.png" alt="A conceptual landscape of many bright rivers rushing through newly opened mountain clefts toward a sunlit sea after a storm" width="1536" height="864" loading="lazy"><figcaption>THE WATERS FIND THEIR COURSES · AI-GENERATED CONCEPTUAL INTERPRETATION</figcaption></figure><p>The victory is therefore more than the defeat of an opponent. The hymn celebrates release: rivers flow, light returns and the mountain no longer seals away what must move.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="resistance"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>06</span><h2>Thunder against thunder</h2></div><p>Vritra does not remain passive. The hymn recalls a counterstroke against Indra and the display of thunder, lightning, mist and hail. Yet these powers do not prevent Indra’s advance.</p><p>The clash is elemental, but the poem measures its outcome through what becomes available again: the waters, the rivers and the open road of the storm.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="question"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>07</span><h2>The hymn turns to a question</h2></div><p>Near its close, the hymn suddenly asks what Indra saw after striking Vritra that made him fearful. It compares his swift crossing of many streams to a falcon’s flight.</p><p>The question complicates a simple portrait of effortless triumph. The text praises Indra’s power while still allowing danger, movement and fear to remain within the encounter.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-chapter" id="sovereignty"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>08</span><h2>The closing praise</h2></div><p>The final verse widens from the battlefield. Indra is praised as ruler over what moves and what stands still, over beings of many kinds and over the people who call upon him.</p><p>The wheel’s rim surrounds its spokes; in the hymn’s closing image, Indra’s sovereignty encompasses the worlds restored to movement. The song ends not at the strike, but with an ordered expanse in which waters flow again.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-reflection" id="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">EDITORIAL REFLECTION / PAUSE &amp; REFLECT</p><h2>What changes when power<br><em>becomes release?</em></h2><p>The hymn joins strength to the opening of what has been confined. Where does courage serve life by restoring movement rather than merely displaying force? This is an editorial invitation, not a translated verse.</p></section>
    <section class="tale-reflection" id="traditions"><p class="eyebrow">TEXT &amp; INTERPRETATION</p><h2>One Rigvedic hymn.<br><em>Not every later Vritra account.</em></h2><p>This reader follows Rigveda 1.32 in Dr Ganga Sahay Sharma’s Hindi edition based on Sayana’s commentary. It retains the hymn’s images of the vajra, mountain passages, confined waters, storm powers and closing sovereignty without turning every verse into a literal modern chronology.</p><p>Later texts tell substantially different stories about Vritra or Vritrasura, including genealogies, moral dilemmas and devotional teachings not present in this hymn. Those details are not inserted here. The figures, clothing, weapon design and landscape in the artwork are contemporary interpretations rather than historical reconstructions.</p><a href="/sources.html#indra-vritra">Read the verse map and edition notes</a></section>
